## Target architecture

### LLM access
All agents call an internal OpenAI-compatible proxy:
- base_url + api_key from env vars (OPENAI_BASE_URL, OPENAI_API_KEY)
- Per-agent model assignment in a single config file (config.py or yaml):
    requirements_analyst: gpt-5.4-nano
    art_director:         gpt-5.4-nano
    data_architect:       gpt-5.4-mini
    api_architect:        gpt-5.4-mini
    frontend_architect:   gpt-5.4-mini
    backend_dev:          gpt-5.2-codex
    frontend_dev:         gpt-5.2-codex
    test_fix:             gpt-5.2-codex

### Agents (OpenAI Agents SDK)
One Agent per current subagent, same names/roles as above. Shared tools
implemented as @function_tool:
- read_file, write_file, edit_file, list_dir — path-sandboxed to the active
  environment directory (reject any path outside it)
- run_bash — shell execution, cwd = environment dir (used for git, dotnet
  build, npm build, booting servers)
- generate_image — wraps the existing generate-image.sh
MCP: attach the currently configured MCP servers (gitlab, playwright,
web-search etc.) via the Agents SDK MCP support (MCPServerStdio). Read the
existing Claude Code MCP config (.mcp.json or claude config) at
EXISTING_C2C_PATH to get the exact server commands, and reuse them.
Playwright MCP is required by the test-fix agent (browser_navigate,
browser_evaluate).

### Temporal
- Workflow: ConceptToCodeWorkflow(env_number: int, requirements_path: str)
- One worker process, one task queue ("concept-to-code"). All activities run
  on this worker (shared local filesystem is required).
- Every agent invocation = one activity. Activities: max_attempts=1,
  heartbeats, start_to_close_timeout 30–60 min for dev/test activities.
- Workflow code is deterministic only: no I/O, no LLM calls, no filesystem.

### Phase mapping (must match current behaviour)
- Phase 0 (activity, no LLM): env setup. Inputs env number (1–8) + req path.
  git fetch / git reset --hard / git checkout -b on BOTH repos (API + UI) in
  ENVIRONMENTS_ROOT/concepttocode-{N}-*, shared branch
  feat/BSSBAT-<YYYYMMDDHHMMSS> (GitLab pre-receive hook requires
  <prefix>/<LETTERS>-<DIGITS>). Smoke-build both to validate templates.
- Phase A: requirements_analyst. In: raw requirements doc. Out:
  docs/normalized-spec.md, gaps filled autonomously with [ASSUMED] decisions.
- Phase B (parallel activities): art_director runs FIRST as a pre-step, then
  data_architect, api_architect, frontend_architect in parallel
  (asyncio.gather over activity handles). Inputs: normalized-spec.md, Global
  Art Direction Ledger, art-direction-library.md. Outputs:
  docs/art-direction.md, docs/data-model.md, docs/api-contracts.md,
  docs/frontend-architecture.md. Then a reconciliation loop (workflow-driven,
  re-invoking architect agents) until all API permissions and routes match
  frontend expectations.
- Phase C+D (parallel activities): backend_dev + frontend_dev. Brief them to
  EXTEND the existing .NET 8 / Next.js scaffolds (e.g. add to Program.cs,
  DataSeeder.cs), never scaffold from scratch. Outputs: code diffs (models,
  controllers, EF InMemory stores, React components, Tailwind configs) and
  generated .png assets (heroes, avatars) in public/images/.
- Phase E (workflow loop, max 5 cycles): test_fix agent activity. Boots
  API/UI servers in background, runs validation matrix — Bash for builds,
  Playwright MCP for UI validation, HTTP fetch for API testing. Errors feed
  back into the next fix cycle. Exit early on green.
- Phase G (activity, no LLM): git add -A, git commit, git push origin
  <branch> on both repos. Never force-push. Do NOT create GitLab MRs.

### Entry point
CLI: python -m c2c.run <env-number> <path-to-requirements.md>
- starts the workflow on the local Temporal server (localhost:7233)
- separate command to run the worker: python -m c2c.worker
- README with setup steps (venv, temporal server start-dev, env vars,
  worker, run command)

